WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.157 --> 00:00:04.950
(reed instrumental music)

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.578
- My great-grandfather
came over to America

00:00:09.585 --> 00:00:13.116
from Northern Iran around
the turn of the 20th century,

00:00:13.116 --> 00:00:15.544
escaping the massacres.

00:00:15.544 --> 00:00:17.972
There was a moment when he was cornered by

00:00:17.972 --> 00:00:20.671
some men that demanded
that he deny his faith

00:00:20.671 --> 00:00:22.804
or they would shoot him, but he refused.

00:00:22.804 --> 00:00:26.708
He said he'd rather die than
deny his faith in Christ.

00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:28.010
And so they pulled the trigger,

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:29.290
but the gun didn't go off.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.458
- My dad came from Hong Kong in the 60's.

00:00:32.458 --> 00:00:34.751
I think the biggest thing
that I don't have to face

00:00:34.751 --> 00:00:36.660
that my dad had to face was

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.026
probably just financial.

00:00:38.026 --> 00:00:40.724
He started off just doing restaurant work.

00:00:40.724 --> 00:00:44.895
He was slowly able to
get a job as an engineer.

00:00:44.895 --> 00:00:46.740
- My grandmother immigrated from Cuba

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.438
to the States in 1967
via the Freedom Flights.

00:00:50.438 --> 00:00:52.369
When Castro came in,

00:00:52.369 --> 00:00:53.873
they had to leave everything behind.

00:00:53.873 --> 00:00:56.048
The moment you made it
known to the government

00:00:56.048 --> 00:00:59.622
that you were planning on
leaving via the Freedom Flights,

00:00:59.622 --> 00:01:01.958
they had someone watching you every day,

00:01:01.958 --> 00:01:03.301
day in and day out.

00:01:03.301 --> 00:01:05.435
So they basically boarded the flight

00:01:05.435 --> 00:01:06.993
with just the clothing on their back

00:01:06.993 --> 00:01:08.966
and I think my mom had a doll.

00:01:08.966 --> 00:01:12.038
- My grandmother immigrated
from Mexico in the 50's.

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:15.793
My grandfather came to
America to make money

00:01:15.793 --> 00:01:17.670
and send it back to the family.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.046
She decided enough was enough

00:01:19.046 --> 00:01:21.041
and the family needed to be together.

00:01:21.041 --> 00:01:24.315
So she sold all of their
earthly possessions

00:01:24.315 --> 00:01:26.161
and sent him a telegram,

00:01:26.161 --> 00:01:29.891
saying "we will be arriving"
and just left with them.

00:01:29.891 --> 00:01:34.317
- My mom came from Ghana
to America in 1979.

00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:36.561
Her father died when she was one

00:01:36.561 --> 00:01:39.142
and her mother died when
she was about 10 years old.

00:01:39.142 --> 00:01:41.521
My mother is someone who's very

00:01:41.521 --> 00:01:43.203
thoughtful and she thinks of everyone

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:45.463
and I think that's always
something I've appreciated

00:01:45.463 --> 00:01:48.450
and I've always hoped that
some time in the future,

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.342
I'd be able to become like that.

00:01:50.342 --> 00:01:52.390
- I am excited.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:55.110
- [Voiceover] Open your eyes.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:56.940
- Whoa, wow.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.302
(laughing) It looks amazing.

00:01:59.302 --> 00:02:01.339
It is kind of a way to like,

00:02:01.339 --> 00:02:04.464
literally stepping into
his shoes kind of a thing.

00:02:04.464 --> 00:02:06.576
It's funny 'cause growing
up, people said that

00:02:06.576 --> 00:02:08.080
I kinda looked like him,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.744
but now I look a lot like him.

00:02:09.744 --> 00:02:12.251
- (laughing) I totally look like my dad.

00:02:12.251 --> 00:02:13.808
These glasses, they really help it.

00:02:13.808 --> 00:02:15.706
I think if you ever needed proof

00:02:15.706 --> 00:02:17.798
that I am my dad's child, then this is it.

00:02:17.798 --> 00:02:20.624
(laughing) It can take
second-generation kids

00:02:20.624 --> 00:02:22.330
a little bit more time than other kids

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.560
to develop that feeling of self-worth.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.035
- (laughing) Oh my God.

00:02:27.035 --> 00:02:29.904
I did not know you could
do this with my hair.

00:02:29.904 --> 00:02:31.163
It's the eyebrows all right,

00:02:31.163 --> 00:02:33.520
my grandma's all about
those dark eyebrows.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.718
It just feels like I've
been through a lot,

00:02:35.718 --> 00:02:37.446
that's what it feels like.

00:02:37.446 --> 00:02:40.208
For me it was a big deal
just moving cross-country,

00:02:40.208 --> 00:02:41.554
but to know that you're never gonna

00:02:41.554 --> 00:02:43.767
see your family again kinda thing

00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:45.857
and no communication whatsoever,

00:02:45.857 --> 00:02:47.745
except for like a phone
call every now and then,

00:02:47.745 --> 00:02:50.860
that's just, what?

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.769
- Oh my gosh!

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:55.819
Oh my gosh, what? (laughing)

00:02:55.819 --> 00:02:57.985
I only know the cultural things

00:02:57.985 --> 00:02:59.895
that she's passed down to me,

00:02:59.895 --> 00:03:02.860
so to be able to wear
this and have a connection

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.927
to not only her, but also my
grandmother, who owned this,

00:03:06.927 --> 00:03:09.081
it's really nice because I don't always

00:03:09.081 --> 00:03:11.151
get to share those things with her.

00:03:11.151 --> 00:03:14.671
- Oh my God! (laughing)

00:03:14.671 --> 00:03:16.419
Wow, oh my gosh.

00:03:16.419 --> 00:03:19.887
See, I'm so emotional
right now. (laughing)

00:03:19.887 --> 00:03:21.945
She's at the end of her life

00:03:21.945 --> 00:03:24.896
and I wish I had appreciated her more

00:03:24.896 --> 00:03:26.176
when I was younger.

00:03:26.176 --> 00:03:28.480
It's hard when you have that like,

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.584
language barrier and you
have the cultural differences

00:03:31.584 --> 00:03:35.083
and knowing the sacrifices
that she made and

00:03:35.083 --> 00:03:36.811
all the things she did for her family,

00:03:36.811 --> 00:03:39.914
I just wish I had understood that

00:03:39.914 --> 00:03:42.331
and appreciated that when I was younger.

00:03:42.331 --> 00:03:45.521
Being an immigrant, for some reason,

00:03:45.521 --> 00:03:49.212
in this time is something
that is dividing us

00:03:49.212 --> 00:03:52.795
when it's actually something
that should be uniting us.

00:03:52.795 --> 00:03:55.068
- It took me a really, really long time

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:57.059
for me to learn that being Asian

00:03:57.059 --> 00:03:59.875
and not being form here was
actually really cool and

00:03:59.875 --> 00:04:03.152
it really informs how I go about my life.

00:04:03.152 --> 00:04:06.391
- You can't let fear stop
you from moving forward

00:04:06.391 --> 00:04:07.895
and making a better life for yourself,

00:04:07.895 --> 00:04:10.313
no matter what the situation you're in.

00:04:10.313 --> 00:04:15.131
(uplifting guitar music)

